This is the classic largest divisible subset problem. Sort the list, then run a DP where dp[i] is the size of the largest chain ending at a[i], with every earlier element in the chain dividing the next one.

Check against the example [1, 3, 6, 13, 17, 18]:
- 1, 3, 6, 18 is a valid chain (1|3, 3|6, 6|18), giving size 4. This matches the expected answer.

Edge cases:
- An empty list returns 0.
- Duplicates: equal numbers divide each other, so a%b==0 holds and they chain together, which is consistent with the pairwise condition.
- Zeros are skipped in the divisibility test to avoid a modulo-by-zero error. If a[j] is 0, then a[i]%0 would fail, and since the list is sorted, zeros come first. I'll handle this by only allowing a[i]%a[j]==0 when a[j]!=0. Zeros form a chain only with other zeros, which I'll ignore as a niche case, so a lone zero still counts as size 1.

